IMO, solving sokoban is one of the easier problems, e.g.:

http://www.clickfest88.freeserve.co.uk/download/

It seems to be licensed using the GPL, so integrating it into a robot 
should be no problem.

Finding minetown would be (relatively) trivial - some simple explore code 
to map each level, then take a down staircase. Where there are two down 
staircases, count the doors on the first level of each branch. The one with 
zero is the mines. There are also plenty of other features (and their 
sounds) which tell you you're not on a mines level, and checking wall shape 
wouldn't be too hard. Mind you, I suspect a robot would be better off going 
to sokoban first.

Much harder would be keeping alive - choosing between fight or flight for 
example, or how to fight. Good pet-handling code would probably be the best 
method to stay alive. Find a single polymorph trap (none are guaranteed 
but the chances are good), and a robot has a very good supply of pets 
useful up to the Castle. Having got to the Castle, even a robot should have 
a reasonable chance of ascension. The tedium of handling pets shouldn't be 
a problem for a robot.

With a careful pet strategy, starvation would probably be the biggest 
killer; careful, pro-active use of prayer would help this (a robot should 
always have a very good idea of its luck, alignment and prayer timeout so 
it could use prayer frequently to make the most of the food it collects).

I rather suspect the biggest challenge for writing robot code would be 
finding creative ways to exploit its low boredom threshold - for example, 
perhaps a robot's first wish (after 3 b?oC from a wand) should be for a 
cursed figurine of a black pudding, if it has seen an unattended altar.


